{
  "delta_from_prior": "Replaces Neon58's disjoint-pair bipartite quotient (4 K_2 x 4 K_2, 16 vars, 48 clauses, which collapses from peak 8 to peak 2 under pair-grouped branching order or Fable73's link-twin contraction) and the prior 18-var 180-clause 3 K_3 x 2 K_3 fixture (peak 4) with a larger, symmetric, overlapping 3-uniform hypergraph transversal contradiction C_8^{(3)} x C_8^{(3)} lifted by four XOR pairs (20 vars, 254 clauses -> exact compression to 16 vars, 84 clauses). Eliminates all disjoint link-twin pairs, moves all 64 cross clauses to width 6 (> IALH k=5) to reduce root IALH windows below Neon58, and aligns uniform block frequency (30) so compress_root's ascending quotient order interleaves both sides to drive predicted peak >= 6-9.",
  "explanation": "### 1. Code Audit of Pinned v5 (`blq-composed-core-scc-emra-ialh5-v5`)\nAuditing all supplied files (`run_counting` / `run_counting_core` / `refute`, `compression.py`, `core_refutation.py`, `check_scc_and_growth.py`, `test_elim.py`, `test_mra.py`) reveals **no correctness bug**:\n- **`compression.py`**: `_verify_certificate` partitions `original` into exact-support blocks covering every original clause (`sum(counts) == len(quotient)`), checks that every referenced basis form is supported inside that block (`forms[i] & ~mask == 0`), exhaustively checks `evaluate(clauses, x) == evaluate(q, image)` on all `2^|support|` local assignments, and verifies `P * L = I_r` over GF(2) entry-by-entry. Surjectivity of `P` plus local-to-global conjunction implies exact equisatisfiability.\n- **`core_refutation.py` & composition**: `probe` enforces `set(core).issubset(set(f))` and only returns `UNSAT` if `compress_root(core)` passes `verify_certificate` with an all-binary (`len(c) <= 2`) quotient refuted by `two_sat(q)`. In `run_counting`, composing `probe` once on an accepted exact quotient preserves soundness. `SAT`/`UNKNOWN` never decides SAT.\n- **Residual solver**: `up_survives` uses unit propagation only; `verify_certificate` independently rebuilds UP tables and checks the global dual via exact `Fraction` arithmetic; `signed_scc`, size-nonincreasing DP `elim`, and subsumption `prune` are all sound.\n- **Priority correction acknowledged**: Neon54/55 K6 near-parity witnesses actually peak 0 because width-5 UP tables retain XOR congruences and the global lattice combines all rows; we do not repeat that error and instead target a genuinely nonlinear, kernel-free, overlapping hypergraph residual.\n\n### 2. New Growth Fixture: XOR-Lifted `C_8^{(3)} x C_8^{(3)}` (20 vars, 254 clauses)\nWe construct a symmetric bipartite hypergraph transversal contradiction on two disjoint tight 3-uniform 8-cycles `C_8^{(3)}`:\n- **Side A** (`a1..a8`): 8 hyperedges of size 3 (`{a_i, a_{i+1}, a_{i+2}}` mod 8). Its **14 minimal transversals** are all subsets with cyclic gaps in `{1,2,3}` and every adjacent gap sum `>= 4`: 8 of size 3 (rotations of `(3,3,2)`: `{a1,a4,a7}, {a2,a5,a8}, ...`), 2 of size 4 (`{a1,a3,a5,a7}, {a2,a4,a6,a8}`), and 4 of size 4 (rotations of `(1,3,1,3)`: `{a1,a2,a5,a6}, {a2,a3,a6,a7}, {a3,a4,a7,a8}, {a4,a5,a8,a1}`). Each gives an all-negative clause.\n- **Side B** (`b1..b8`): isomorphic `C_8^{(3)}` with 8 hyperedges and 14 all-negative minimal transversal clauses.\n- **Cross clauses**: all `8 * 8 = 64` positive width-6 clauses `e_A union e_B` (`e_A in E(C_8^{(3)})`, `e_B in E(C_8^{(3)})`).\n- **UNSAT proof**: Any assignment satisfying the 14 negative clauses on Side A cannot cover all 8 hyperedges of `C_8^{(3)}`, leaving at least one all-0 hyperedge `e_A`. Symmetrically, Side B has at least one all-0 hyperedge `e_B`, falsifying `e_A union e_B`.\n- **XOR lifting (`16 -> 20` vars)**: Map unlifted variables interleaved as `a1=1, b1=2, a2=3, b2=4, a3=5, b3=6, a5=7, b5=8, a6=9, b6=10, a8=11, b8=12`, and substitute four non-adjacent variables at cyclic distance 3: `a4 = x13 XOR x14`, `b4 = x15 XOR x16`, `a7 = x17 XOR x18`, `b7 = x19 XOR x20`. Because `a4, a7` never co-occur in any 3-hyperedge (and likewise `b4, b7`), every cross clause lifts to width 6, 7, or 8 (`14 * 14 = 196` cross clauses) and each side's 14 negative blocks lift to `29` clauses (widths 3\u20136), totaling **254 clauses**, max width **8**.\n\n### 3. Expected Behavior on Pinned v5 (Discovery vs. Residual Growth)\n- **Exact root compression succeeds**: All 92 exact-support blocks have width `<= 8`, total space points `13,216 <= 16,384`, and verification work `< 1,000,000 << 8,000,000`. Every intended basis form appears in exactly `24 + 6 = 30` blocks (strictly exceeding any non-basis sum, which appears in `<= 18` blocks), so greedy basis selection is exact and compatible, compressing `20 vars / 254 clauses` cleanly to `16 vars / 84 clauses` with ascending quotient order `a1, b1, a2, b2, a3, b3, a5, b5, a6, b6, a8, b8, a4, b4, a7, b7`.\n- **Core shortcut returns `UNKNOWN` at both stages**: In the original formula every block is a single complete coset of its stabilizer (`len(z) in {1,2,4}`), and in the quotient every block is a singleton (`len(z) == 1`); `probe` removes 0 clauses (`no_clauses_removed`) on both.\n- **Root IALH-5 returns `UNKNOWN` and stays fast**: All 64 cross clauses have width `6 > k=5`, yielding **0 cross windows** at the root and only ~92 negative windows total (fewer than Neon58's 112). For every window of size `<= 5`, `0` and all standard basis vectors `e_i` survive `up_survives` (every negative clause has width `>= 3`), so `hull_rows` yields 0 constraints.\n- **Residual growth (`peak >= 4`, predicted 6\u20139)**: At Layer 1 (`a1`), both `a1=0` and `a1=1` survive with 0 DP eliminations (`2` states). Layer 2 (`b1`) yields `4` states. At Layer 3 (`a2`), `a1=1` splits into 2 surviving branches (`a2=0` and `a2=1`) while `a1=0` retains `a2=1`, giving `6` states; Layer 4 (`b2`) symmetrically expands the `b1=1` states to `9` states.\n\n### 4. Challenge to Fable's Nonlinear Proposals & Scope\nUnlike Neon58's `4 K_2 x 4 K_2`, `C_8^{(3)} x C_8^{(3)}` has **zero disjoint link-twin pairs**: every vertex belongs to 3 overlapping hyperedges and 6 overlapping minimal transversals, so Fable73's link-twin contraction (`x OR y`) and pair-grouping order heuristics do not apply. Dropping any single cross clause yields a nearby SAT control (setting its 6 variables to 0 and all remaining variables to 1? No\u2014setting the two 3-hyperedges to 0 and covering the remaining 5 vertices per side with a non-transversal, e.g. 2 ones per side), illustrating why local/linear invariants cannot separate SAT from UNSAT here. This is a finite 20-variable growth fixture, not a universal asymptotic lower bound.",
